--TEST--
`Core\Taint::assertTrustedBytes` removes `tainted` from `bytes` and nothing else: a `secret` operand and a `string` operand are refused, while `tainted bytes` and plain `bytes` pass — `rule:security/assert-trusted`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The accepted side first, so the refusals below are a bound with both sides
// named rather than a member that refuses everything.
tainted bytes $tb = "t" as tainted bytes;
bytes $plain = "p" as bytes;
bytes $a = Core\Taint::assertTrustedBytes($tb, "the test wrote it");
bytes $b = Core\Taint::assertTrustedBytes($plain, "already plain");

// Confidentiality is a separate axis: a `secret` passes `revealBytes` first.
secret tainted bytes $stb = "s" as bytes;
bytes $c = Core\Taint::assertTrustedBytes($stb, "a secret is not laundered here");

// The `string` base has its own member, `assertTrusted`.
tainted string $ts = "x";
bytes $d = Core\Taint::assertTrustedBytes($ts, "the wrong base");
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `secret tainted bytes`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted string`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
